Both are verbatim copies of the Free Software Foundation's own documents.
This file is the project's own summary; where the two disagree, the license
texts govern.

The grant above covers the whole work.  Most source files also carry a
machine-readable `SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later` tag, which is
the per-file form of the same grant and travels with a file lifted out of
the tree.  The tag is not yet universal: the SQL migrations, the HTML
templates, the build files (CMakeLists.txt, Dockerfile, docker-compose.yml,
run.sh) and the documentation carry no tag and are covered only by this
file.  That is a labelling gap, not a licensing one, and tagging them is
worth doing.


Why "or later" is not decoration
--------------------------------

Version 3 is not hypothetical here.  The daemon links OpenSSL 3.x, which is
Apache-2.0, and Apache-2.0 cannot be combined with version 2 alone: its
patent-termination and indemnification clauses are "further restrictions"
of the kind GPLv2 section 6 forbids.  Version 3 was written to accommodate
exactly those clauses.

The mechanism is an option, not a relicense.  Per GPLv2 section 9 the choice
belongs to whoever exercises the rights, not to us: these files stay
GPL-2.0-or-later and their SPDX tags never become GPL-3.0.  What "or later"
does is make a compliant path exist where under GPL-2.0-only none did.

Three consequences follow, and each is easier to state now than to discover
later.

  - It was never the copyright holders who were blocked.  Section 6 binds
    licensees; an owner conveying their own work needs no license from
    themselves.  What GPL-2.0-only did was hand everyone else a work they
    could build but could not lawfully redistribute once linked.  The
    relicense fixes that for them, not for us.

  - Anyone who does convey a linked binary is choosing version 3 in
    practice, because the version 2 branch of the option hits the same wall
    as before.  The source tree is GPL-2.0-or-later; a conveyed binary that
    links OpenSSL is effectively GPL-3.0-or-later.  Both are true at once.

  - The option only works forwards.  Copies already published under
    GPL-2.0-only -- including everything on upstream/master and the existing
    release tags -- keep the terms they were given.

Two things this does not fix.  Nothing in the build pins OpenSSL >= 3.0
(`find_package(OpenSSL ...)` at CMakeLists.txt, src/gwdiscord/CMakeLists.txt
and web/cmake/FindMySQL.cmake all take any version), and OpenSSL 1.1.1 and
earlier carry the old dual OpenSSL/SSLeay license, whose advertising clause
is incompatible with version 2 and version 3 alike.  And "or later" leaves
the version 2 arm unusable for linked binaries, which an explicit OpenSSL
linking exception would restore; adding one needs both copyright holders,
which will never be easier than it is now.


Third-party components
----------------------

None of the following is relicensed by this project; each keeps its own
terms, and a binary distribution must carry them.

    TDLib (submodules/td)                BSL-1.0
    MySQL Connector/C++                  GPL-2.0 WITH Universal-FOSS-Exception-1.0
    libmysqlclient (via the above)       GPL-2.0 WITH Universal-FOSS-Exception-1.0
    Drogon (submodules/drogon)           MIT
    trantor (within drogon)              BSD-3-Clause
    JsonCpp (via drogon)                 MIT
    MariaDB Connector/C                  LGPL-2.1-or-later
    OpenSSL 3.x                          Apache-2.0
    libsodium                            ISC
    libcurl                              curl (MIT-like)
    zlib                                 Zlib
    Boost (Beast/Asio, header-only)      BSL-1.0
    libstdc++                            GPL-3.0-or-later WITH GCC-exception-3.1
    nlohmann/json (web/third_party)      MIT
    inja (web/third_party)               MIT
    jQuery, flatpickr, lottie, select2   MIT (texts in web/static/*.LICENSE)
    stopwords-iso/stopwords-id           MIT (data in web/src/mcp/Stopwords.hpp)

libcurl pulls in the distribution's TLS, resolver and authentication stack
(GnuTLS, nettle, GMP, libidn2, libpsl, libssh, Kerberos and others).  Those
are unmodified operating-system packages under their own terms and are not
enumerated here; a binary distribution that bundles them must carry theirs.

One entry is load-bearing and would be hard to reconstruct.  MySQL
Connector/C++ and libmysqlclient are GPL-2.0-**only** by Oracle's explicit
election ("Oracle elects to use only the General Public License version 2
(GPLv2) at this time", submodules/mysql-connector-cpp/LICENSE.txt), and
GPLv2-only code cannot be combined with GPLv3.  What makes the combination
lawful is Oracle's Universal FOSS Exception v1.0, clause (iii), which
permits linking with other FSF-free software "after enabling
interoperability using the Interfaces of one or both, and distributing the
resulting combination under different licenses for the respective portions
thereof."

That clause applies here because src/mysql/ uses the connector through its
public headers only and links it dynamically.  Two conditions come with it.
Copying connector implementation code into first-party files, or vendoring
it statically, would take the exception away.  And the exception defines the
other side as software distributed *with complete corresponding source*, so
the version 3 election above holds only while source accompanies any binary
-- a precondition that did not exist under GPL-2.0-only, where GPLv2
section 2(b) propagated natively and the exception was never needed.


What is and is not distributed
------------------------------

Today this project distributes source only, through its git remotes.  The
`tgloggerd:latest` container image is a toolchain: it carries the compiler
and the runtime libraries, and none of this project's code.  The source tree
and build outputs are bind-mounted from the host at run time, so conveying
that image conveys no part of the Program and triggers no obligation under
this license.  Publishing a built binary or an image that contains one is a
different act, and the paragraphs above are what govern it.


Separable sub-projects
----------------------

src/gwdiscord and src/gwmcp are self-contained libraries that happen to live
in this repository.  Both were GPL-2.0-or-later before the rest of the tree
was, both name a single copyright holder rather than two, and each carries
its own copies of the license texts so that lifting the directory out takes
its terms with it.
